@@ -0,0 +1,162 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `zip` (zip-rs/zip2 @ 1058f80, v9.0.0-pre2)
+
+## 1. Summary
+
+Added 10 hegel property-based tests across three existing integration-test
+files (`tests/end_to_end.rs`, `tests/invalid_path.rs`,
+`tests/prepended_garbage.rs`), covering the write→read roundtrip with drawn
+entry names / contents / compression methods (Stored, Deflated, Bzip2, Zstd),
+name-lookup and metadata consistency, comment and empty-archive roundtrips,
+duplicate-name rejection, large (200–400 KB) entries, path-traversal safety of
+`enclosed_name`/`mangled_name`, and — the key robustness target — no-panic
+behavior of `ZipArchive::new` plus full bounded entry reads on arbitrary
+bytes, crafted EOCD / Zip64-locator records with drawn count/size/offset
+fields, and once-valid archives corrupted by byte overwrites, truncation, or
+targeted EOCD-field rewrites. **No bugs found** — fully green at default case
+counts and at a counter-verified 10x exploratory run. This matches
+expectations for a crate that ships two AFL fuzz harnesses (`fuzz/read`,
+`fuzz/write` with a large `Arbitrary` operation model) and has had multiple
+CVE-driven hardening rounds; the added value is a fast, shrinkable,
+CI-friendly regression net over the same attack surface, plus roundtrip
+dimensions the fuzzers don't assert (byte-for-byte content equality,
+metadata/CRC consistency against an independent oracle).
+
+Setup notes: `cargo add --dev hegeltest` (0.28.2) worked directly on
+Rust 1.95 / edition 2024 (crate MSRV 1.88 — no `--ignore-rust-version`
+needed); `crc32fast` was also added as a dev-dependency to serve as an
+independent CRC oracle (it is already in the crate's runtime dependency
+tree). No maintainer AI-consent gate was found (no `HUMAN_VS_MACHINE.md`,
+no `.ai-ack`, no anti-AI clause in `CONTRIBUTING.md`/`README.md`/license).
+Nothing was pushed; work is committed locally only.
+
+## 2. Properties Added
+
+`tests/end_to_end.rs` — `mod hegel_props` (generalizes the fixed-entries
+`end_to_end` test; shared `entry_name` composite draws 1–3 `/`-joined
+components mixing arbitrary Unicode `text()` with awkward samples — `..`,
+`.`, `café`, `☃`, `🐢`, quotes, tabs, RTL override, trailing dots — with
+trailing `/`/`\` stripped because those bytes mark *directory* entries per
+`spec::is_dir`; `archive_entries` draws unique names, 0–1024-byte contents,
+and a per-entry compression method from the feature-enabled set):
+
+1. `prop_write_read_roundtrip` — **crown roundtrip**: build an archive in
+   memory with `ZipWriter`, finalize, reopen with `ZipArchive`; entry count
+   preserved, and every entry's name and bytes match both **by index** (in
+   write order) and **by name**. Covers the empty archive (0 entries) and
+   empty file entries (0 bytes), since both generators reach zero.
+2. `prop_name_lookup_consistency` — `index_for_name`, `name_for_index`, and
+   `file_names()` agree with each other and with what was written, in write
+   order; `len()`/`is_empty()` preserved.
+3. `prop_entry_metadata_consistency` — read-back `size()` equals written
+   length, `crc32()` equals the independent `crc32fast::hash` oracle,
+   `compression()` equals the method requested, `is_file()`/`!is_dir()`.
+4. `prop_empty_archive_with_comment_roundtrip` — zero-entry archive with a
+   drawn 0–1000-byte comment reopens empty with the comment preserved
+   byte-for-byte; one branch splices an EOCD magic (`PK\x05\x06`) into the
+   comment to stress the backwards magic search that locates the real EOCD.
+5. `prop_duplicate_name_rejected` — the second `start_file` with an
+   already-used name errors (documented contract), and the archive still
+   finalizes with the original entry's bytes intact.
+6. `prop_large_entry_roundtrip` (`test_cases = 15`) — a 200–400 KB entry
+   (drawn repeated chunk) roundtrips byte-for-byte under every enabled
+   method, crossing internal buffer boundaries.
+
+`tests/invalid_path.rs` — `mod hegel_path_props` (generalizes the
+example-based traversal tests):
+
+7. `prop_sanitized_names_never_escape` — for archives whose entry names are
+   drawn from a hostile-name generator (1–6 components biased toward `..`,
+   `.`, drive prefixes `C:`, `~`, `$HOME`, NUL-containing components, drawn
+   `/` or `\` separator, optional absolute prefix, plus arbitrary Unicode):
+   every `enclosed_name()` that returns `Some` is relative with only
+   `Normal`/`CurDir` components, and every `mangled_name()` is relative with
+   only `Normal` components — i.e. neither can escape an extraction root.
+
+`tests/prepended_garbage.rs` — `mod hegel_robustness_props` (untrusted-bytes
+robustness; shared `drain_untrusted` exercises `ZipArchive::new`, comment/
+offset access, per-entry name/`enclosed_name`/`mangled_name`, capped
+decompression of every entry, and the streaming `read_zipfile_from_stream`
+path — every step may error, nothing may panic; reads are capped at
+`1 << 24` bytes per entry, mirroring `fuzz/read`'s `MAX_BYTES_TO_READ`, a
+bound protecting the *test's* resources rather than asserting a library
+contract):
+
+8. `prop_arbitrary_bytes_never_panic` — drawn from three branches: raw
+   arbitrary bytes; junk + a syntactically plausible EOCD whose disk
+   numbers, entry counts, CD size, and CD offset are fully drawn (the
+   classic attacker-controlled fields); junk + a Zip64 EOCD locator with a
+   drawn (possibly self-referential) u64 offset followed by an
+   all-sentinel EOCD, forcing the Zip64 path.
+9. `prop_corrupted_archive_never_panics` (`test_cases = 200`) — build a
+   valid 1–4-entry archive, then corrupt it one of three ways: 1–8 drawn
+   byte overwrites biased toward the tail (EOCD + central directory);
+   truncation at a drawn position; or **targeted EOCD-field rewrites**
+   (entry counts, CD size, CD offset, comment length overwritten with drawn
+   values while the rest of the archive stays valid — headers that lie).
+   Open + bounded full read never panics.
+10. `prop_prepended_junk_preserves_contents` — generalizes the existing
+    `test_prepended_garbage`: ANY drawn junk prefix (0–512 bytes, including
+    bytes containing PK magics) before a valid archive leaves entry count,
+    contents, and `offset()` (== junk length) intact — the documented
+    self-extracting-archive support.
+
+## 3. Real Bugs Found (pinned, deliberately failing)
+
+None. No panics, hangs, OOMs, roundtrip violations, metadata inconsistencies,
+or traversal escapes were observed at default or 10x case counts. No
+`#[ignore]`d reproducers were needed (no process-aborting behavior was
+encountered).
+
+## 4. Verification (Run and Reflect)
+
+- **Liveness**: every assertion-bearing property (all except the two pure
+  no-panic properties) was mutated (assertion inverted) in a single pass;
+  all 8 mutations failed with minimal shrunk counterexamples (e.g. the crown
+  roundtrip shrank to `[("0", [], Stored)]`, the traversal property to
+  `["..."]` — confirming the guarded `if let Some(p) = enclosed_name()`
+  branch fires). All mutations were then reverted and the suite re-ran green.
+- **Guarded-branch coverage of `drain_untrusted`**: temporary `eprintln!`
+  instrumentation counted, per 100/200 cases: for corrupted archives —
+  40 successful opens, 86 successful `by_index` entries, 285 streaming
+  entries (all three paths live). For raw arbitrary bytes the open path
+  never succeeded (0/100) — expected, since random bytes almost never form
+  a valid EOCD; that is precisely why property 9's corrupted-valid-archive
+  generator and the targeted EOCD-field branch exist: they drive the same
+  drain function through the deep read paths with lying headers.
+- **10x exploratory run**: all properties temporarily raised 10x
+  (100→1000, 200→2000, 15→100) with per-case counters; counters confirmed
+  1000–2065 cases per property actually executed (not a silent no-op run).
+  All green; counts then reverted to defaults.
+- The existing example-based tests in all three files still pass, as does
+  the crate's full default-feature test suite.
+
+## 5. Exclusions, Limitations, and Follow-ups
+
+- **Compression methods**: the roundtrip set is Stored/Deflated/Bzip2/Zstd
+  per the task brief (all default features). Lzma, Xz, Ppmd, and legacy
+  methods are written by the crate too and could be added to
+  `roundtrip_methods()` for free; Deflate64 is read-only and AES encryption
+  needs password plumbing — both excluded.
+- **Directory-marker names**: `entry_name` strips trailing `/`/`\` because
+  those bytes make `start_file` produce something `is_dir()` reports as a
+  directory — directory entries belong to `add_directory`. The hostile-name
+  generator in the traversal property deliberately does *not* strip them.
+- **Read caps are resource bounds, not assertions**: a corrupted small
+  archive can legitimately inflate far beyond its input size (deflate
+  ~1000:1, bzip2 more), so the robustness tests cap at 16 MiB per entry and
+  copy to `io::sink()` without asserting the cap is unreached; exact-size
+  assertions live in the roundtrip properties where sizes are known.
+- **Vacuous-open on pure noise**: `ZipArchive::new` on raw random bytes
+  essentially always errors (measured 0/100 opens), so that branch of
+  property 8 only tests EOCD-search rejection; deep-path coverage comes
+  from the corrupted-archive property (measured live, see section 4).
+- **Not covered** (candidates for follow-up): `ZipWriter::new_append` /
+  merge/copy operations under drawn operation sequences (the write fuzzer's
+  domain — a hegel state machine over `FileOperation`-like ops would port
+  it); encryption roundtrips (AES/ZipCrypto); extraction to disk
+  (`extract()` touches the filesystem; kept out of property tests);
+  `by_path`/`root_dir` semantics; extra-field roundtrips.
+- Tests are `#[cfg(not(miri))]` — the crate runs its suite under Miri in CI,
+  and hegel's engine plus hundreds of archive builds per test are
+  impractical there; the surrounding files use the same convention for
+  heavy tests.
